From 036ed9f6368963d4ed0e7a660494ceb3df24af07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 23:43:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/21] l2r-local: step D (R2): Nat and Int at polyffi type
 arguments are an owned handle, reussir_rt::rcgmp::Mpz

#795 lets `Nat` and `Int` cross `#[ffi(import)]` as the raw rcgmp handle,
`*mut c_void`, which the wrapper releases after the body: the body borrows.
The Rust renderer spells them that way everywhere, and the wrapper decides
the release from the substituted parameter type. Both break where a texture
holds an integer as its own:

- a type argument: `Vec<Nat>` is `Vec<*mut c_void>`, so the vector never
  releases its elements (a leak) and clones without retaining;
- a generic `x : T` at T = Nat is released after the body, although the
  body stored it (`v.push(x)`) or returned it: the vector or the caller
  then holds a freed handle. Under ASan, a `Vec<Nat>` of heap values pushed
  and read back is a heap-use-after-free.

lean2rr needs these for typed arrays, cells and once-cells at Nat/Int under
the rcgmp backend (step D), and Reussir's own std collections hit them at
Nat.

New `reussir_rt::rcgmp::Mpz`: a `#[repr(transparent)]` pointer that owns
one nonatomic reference. `Clone` calls `rcgmp_mpz_retain(p, 0)`, `Drop`
calls `rcgmp_mpz_release(p, 0)`, `PartialEq`/`Ord` call `rcgmp_mpz_cmp`,
and `from_raw`, `into_raw`, `as_ptr` convert to and from the raw handle.
So the runtime's BTreeSet/BTreeMap order Nat keys by value, and its
HashMap/HashSet, which take the digest from Reussir's hashers, compare
them by value. Mpz has no Hash: a Rust texture that needs `T: Hash` at Nat
now fails to compile, where before it silently hashed the pointers. Every
method that calls the rcgmp runtime is `#[inline]`, so it is compiled only
into the textures that use it: libreussir_rt (.a, .so, the ASan variant,
release and debug) references no rcgmp symbol, and a program without
integers still links without the rcgmp runtime. The crate has no rcgmp
feature to gate on (its rcgmp allocation hooks are unconditional), so none
is added.

The renderer (`full/ffi.rs`) spells `Nat`/`Int` as `::reussir_rt::rcgmp::Mpz`
at every position, and an `#[ffi(import)]` parameter or result keeps the
raw handle only where the signature declares `Nat` or `Int` itself
(`boundary_rust_name`, `raw_rcgmp_handle`); monomorphization (`full/mono.rs`)
now reads the declared types next to the substituted ones. So a declared
`Nat` parameter is still borrowed and released after the body (#795
unchanged), while a generic parameter at Nat is the body's own `Mpz` with
no release after the body, and a generic result at Nat moves its reference
to the caller (the C ABI still passes a pointer). Placeholders `[:T:]` and
opaque instances (`Vec<Mpz>` and its drop hook) follow.

Tests: frontend/bigint_ffi_std.rr (std's copy-on-write Vec, HashMap,
HashSet, BTreeMap, BTreeSet, VecDeque and BinaryHeap at Nat and Int with
heap values, std built as a package) and its ASan twin
sanitizer/bigint-ffi-std-asan.rr, whose textures compile against the
sanitized runtime's crates (new lit substitution %polyffi_asan_libdirs,
from CMake's REUSSIR_RT_ASAN_DEPS), frontend/bigint_ffi_edge.rr and its
ASan twin sanitizer/bigint-ffi-edge-asan.rr (a nested Vec<Vec<Nat>>,
unused and twice-used generic parameters, a declared Nat among generic
parameters in the packed boundary, a declared Nat result of a generic
texture, a closure moving the handle, one heap value passed twice, a
vector dropped while it holds heap elements, Int ordering across signs in
a BTreeSet); with the l2r-base5 compiler both give wrong results and uses
after free. frontend/bigint_ffi_generic.rr (a Rust Vec<Nat> with heap and
immediate elements: push, get, set, a copy-on-write clone on the Rust
side, growth to 100 elements; a generic texture returning one argument and
dropping the others, at Nat and Int, also through the four-parameter
packed boundary; a declared Nat parameter stored by the body and a
declared Nat result; a BTreeSet<Nat> deduplicating equal heap values; the
rendered spellings) and its ASan twin sanitizer/bigint-ffi-generic-asan.rr
(leaks, double frees and uses after free); a reussir-core unit test of the
spellings; a reussir-rt unit test of the handle's layout and raw round
trip. docs/design/polymorphic-ffi.md describes both spellings.
